      "dom_id": "question-72032",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "Solve $-{y}^{3}+3{y}^{2}+2y-6=0.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we multiply the equation by $-1$ to make the coefficient of the cubic term positive. This gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{3}-3{y}^{2}-2y+6=0.\n$$\n\nWe then proceed to factor the left-hand side by grouping:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{3}-3{y}^{2}-2y+6 & = \\\\ ({y}^{3}-3{y}^{2})+(-2y+6) & = \\\\ {y}^{2}(y-3)-2(y-3) & = \\\\ ({y}^{2}-2)(y-3) & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve $({y}^{2}-2)(y-3)=0$ by setting each factor equal to $0.$\n\n- Setting the factor $({y}^{2}-2)$ equal to $0,$ we get $y=\\pm \\sqrt{2}.$\n\n- Setting the factor $(y-3)$ equal to $0,$ we get $y=3.$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $y=3,\\,\\pm \\sqrt{2}.$",
